      "The so-called browser smoke is a jsdom/component test, not real browser automation. That is weaker than the plan language implies.",
      "The aws-profile CI case overclaims coverage because taskInbox.test.tsx injects LOCAL_RUNTIME_CONFIG and therefore does not actually exercise aws runtime resolution inside the component.",
      "Important negative paths are largely unproven: mark-read failure rollback, retry failure behavior, and actual navigation integration."
    ]
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC are broadly aligned on file inventory, runtime env vars, and the intended FastAPI routes.",
      "Docs claim 'apertura del dettaglio documento' and target-runtime readiness more strongly than the code proves. The emitted source only offers callback integration or URL mutation, not real routed opening.",
      "LTC/aws-profile smoke suggests the same UI is exercised under aws runtime configuration, but the included UI test bypasses env-driven runtime resolution by passing LOCAL_RUNTIME_CONFIG directly.",
      "The packaging repeatedly uses 'browser smoke' wording, while the test is Testing Library on jsdom rather than true browser automation."
    ]
  },
  "promotion_fit": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Module placement follows the planned namespace structure and stays within the allowed frontend/shared families.",
      "The implementation is localized enough for later promotion into canonical src/tests targets.",
      "Promotion fit is weakened by self-contained app seams: router integration is not canonical yet, and QueryClientProvider ownership is embedded inside the screen rather than clearly aligned with app-level composition.",
      "The candidate is a workable base for hardening, but not yet close enough to promotion to justify an optimistic verdict."
    ]
  },
  "shared_common_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "Shared extraction of WorkflowStateBadge into frontend.app.shared.ui is justified because workflow state rendering is likely cross-slice UI.",
      "The runtime helper under shared.query is lightweight and coherent with the lane conventions, though it currently has only one visible consumer and is somewhat early for shared extraction.",
      "No obvious parallel or duplicate shared/common module family was introduced."
    ]
  },
  "dual_mode_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable_with_gaps",
    "notes": [
      "The design preserves one business/UI model across local and aws modes, with differences expressed through runtime configuration rather than a separate local-only architecture.",
      "The same DTO shape and HTTP route family are used for both modes, which is the correct design direction.",
      "Verification is weaker than claimed: aws-mode component behavior is not meaningfully exercised beyond runtime config parsing, so dual-mode integrity is conceptually sound but under-proven."
    ]
  },
  "blockers": [
    "Document open behavior is not truly implemented as promotable routing; the fallback only mutates the URL with history.pushState and does not prove detail-screen opening.",
    "Tests do not validate the real HTTP adapter contract or fetch/error behavior, leaving key integration seams unproven.",
    "CI/docs overclaim aws-profile and browser-smoke coverage relative to what the emitted tests actually execute.",
    "Promotion readiness is overstated because the staged component still depends on host-app seams for the most visible user action."
  ],
  "hardening_targets": [
    "Prove the 'open document detail' acceptance path with promotable router integration or an equivalent canonical navigation seam that renders actual detail navigation behavior.",
    "Add focused tests for createHttpWorkflowInboxApi covering request URLs, HTTP methods, credentials, 204 handling, and error-envelope parsing.",
    "Add negative-path tests for mark-read failure, optimistic rollback behavior, retry failure messaging, and empty/error query states.",
    "Make aws-profile coverage real by exercising component runtime resolution without injecting LOCAL_RUNTIME_CONFIG in the supposed aws-profile smoke path.",
    "Align README/KIT/HOWTO/LTC wording with the actual test scope, especially around 'browser smoke' and target-runtime claims."
  ]
}
